course: dictionary
lesson: 5792
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Lunch was bacon and broad beans, and a macaroni pudding.» Pudding and lunch point to food, so macaroni here means a kind of pasta.
explain: In the old song, «He stuck a feather in his cap and called it macaroni.» A feather cannot be pasta, so the word must mean something else: a young man who thought he looked very fine.
explain: In the 1700s, a macaroni was a young man in very fancy, showy clothes and a big wig, copying the fashions of Europe. British soldiers sang the song to mock the Americans, so the word was used as a joke about a boastful, fashionable gentleman.
fact: Macaroni is a kind of pasta in short, curved tubes.
fact: Macaroni is uncountable when it means pasta: you say some macaroni, not two macaronis.
fact: In the 18th century, a macaroni was a young man who wore very fancy, showy clothes and a big wig.
fact: The old plural for the fashion meaning was macaronies.
fact: Food words such as pudding and cheese point to pasta, while clothes, wigs and feathers point to the fashion meaning.
exercise: «Lunch was bacon and broad beans, and a macaroni pudding.» What is macaroni in this sentence? | *A kind of pasta in short, curved tubes | A feather for a cap | A young man in fancy clothes | A type of bean
exercise: The song says, «He stuck a feather in his cap and called it macaroni.» What does macaroni mean in this song? | A sort of hat for rain | A kind of soup | A short nap | *A fashionable young man who thought he was a fine gentleman
exercise: Which sentence uses macaroni as food? | *We ate macaroni cheese for tea | He wore a big wig and showy clothes | Badger took forty winks | The soldiers sang a song
test: In «a macaroni pudding», what clue tells you macaroni is food? | *The word pudding | Europe | A wig | A feather
test: Why do we say «some macaroni» for the pasta and not «two macaronis»? | It is a song word | Macaroni is always a plural word | *Macaroni is uncountable when it means pasta | Macaroni means a young man, so it is counted
exam: In «called it macaroni», what does macaroni mean? | Pasta in short, curved tubes | *A young man in very fancy, showy clothes | A feather in a cap | A kind of song
